THE RISKS OF AN UNENCRYPTED BBS
and what it means for your privacy

Four short pages. They take a minute,
and they are the minute worth spending.

TELNET IS NOT ENCRYPTED

Telnet has no encryption. It never has,
and on this board it never will.

Everything you type crosses the network
as readable text. What you say, and the
password you type to get in.

That is not an oversight. It is the
price of letting a 1982 computer call,
and a C64 cannot do encryption. Better
to tell you than quietly pretend.

Page 1 of 4
WHAT THAT ACTUALLY RISKS

Reading your password needs two things:
a sniffer, which is any program that
records network traffic, and a position
on the path between you and this board.

Who has that? Whoever runs the wifi you
are on. Whoever runs the office
network. Your internet provider, and
the board's. Somebody who has put
themselves in the middle on purpose.

Not a stranger on the internet, then.
It takes access, and most people simply
do not have it.

Low risk. Not no risk. Worth one unique
password, not an afternoon of worry.

Page 2 of 4
YOUR PASSWORD ON THIS BOARD

It is never stored as you typed it. It
is salted and hashed with SHA-256, a
thousand rounds, and only the result is
written down. Nobody can read it back,
including the sysop.

That protects the file if the file is
stolen. It does nothing for the wire,
where you typed it in the clear.

And a hash is not magic. A common
password still falls to a lookup table.

Page 3 of 4
WHAT THIS BOARD KNOWS

The sysop sees your handle, the address
you called from, when you called and for
how long, and the last command you ran.
Staff can watch a node. Messages you
leave sit in a file until they are read.

Assume whoever owns the machine can read
what is on it. That is true everywhere.
Here you at least know who they are.

Honestly? It is a hobby board on a five
dollar chip, and it is conversation.

Use a password you use nowhere else.

